00:05:32.942 --> 00:05:34.150
Can we go back to the slides?

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:41.450
MARTIN OMANDER:
So ops automation,

00:05:41.450 --> 00:05:43.220
one might think it's
about computers,

00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:46.800
but I'm here to tell
you it's about humans.

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:50.390
And we all have our different
views of automation.

00:05:50.390 --> 00:05:53.150
Junior James, he is a
pretty junior developer at

00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:54.020
Into the Woods.

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:57.620
He just wants to avoid
frustration and bureaucracy,

00:05:57.620 --> 00:06:00.410
just bang out his code
and deploy it and have

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:04.550
it be tested and have it work.

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:06.020
Process Pari.

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:09.380
Pari, she's into
processes and scripts.

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:11.570
She has this idea
of ops automation

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:16.340
should be about reducing
manual toil for the developers.

00:06:16.340 --> 00:06:19.940
Teamwork Tess, she
focuses on teamwork,

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:23.810
and she wants everybody to
have confidence in the code

00:06:23.810 --> 00:06:26.070
and bring relief to the team.

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:28.640
And finally, the fourth
person in the engineering team

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:30.050
here is Sang.

00:06:30.050 --> 00:06:31.310
He's the CTO.

00:06:31.310 --> 00:06:33.590
He wants to make it
really clear to everybody

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:36.530
what they're supposed to
do as part of a release.

00:06:36.530 --> 00:06:39.230
And we'll see these
four engineers here.

00:06:39.230 --> 00:06:43.340
They are going to discuss
and build a CI/CD pipeline,

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:47.250
and we'll see the final
result at the end of the talk.

00:06:47.250 --> 00:06:49.470
So they will have to go
through various things.

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:52.520
There are six major
decisions that they

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:55.730
will need to make from
repo layout, all the way

00:06:55.730 --> 00:06:57.020
to security.

00:06:57.020 --> 00:07:01.520
And they'll discuss, see pros
and cons of various approaches,

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:05.210
and arrive at a
solution for each one.

00:07:05.210 --> 00:07:08.150
Repo layout, first one--

00:07:08.150 --> 00:07:09.830
it's foundational.

00:07:09.830 --> 00:07:11.720
Before you do this,
you can't really

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:13.740
do any of the other pieces.

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:16.740
There are various ways you
can do repo management.

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:18.710
So for example,
Junior James, he's

00:07:18.710 --> 00:07:21.950
heard a lot about microservices
and being super agile,

00:07:21.950 --> 00:07:25.220
and he's like one repo
per microservice, right?

00:07:25.220 --> 00:07:30.140
They keep everything light
and really truly separated,

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:31.872
these microservices.

00:07:31.872 --> 00:07:35.210
Pari notes that there is
some integration tests.

00:07:35.210 --> 00:07:36.770
Where would they live?

00:07:36.770 --> 00:07:40.190
Tess notes that there are
some code calling each other

00:07:40.190 --> 00:07:41.450
across microservices.

00:07:41.450 --> 00:07:45.350
Where would the API
specs for that live?

00:07:45.350 --> 00:07:49.010
Also, Pari right notes
that sometimes they

00:07:49.010 --> 00:07:51.920
need to run shared tools
across the codebase.

00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:56.600
That's really easier with a
monorepo, single code base.

00:07:56.600 --> 00:07:58.850
And this is, of course,
easier to sharing knowledge.

00:07:58.850 --> 00:08:02.130
So after some discussion,
James is convinced,

00:08:02.130 --> 00:08:05.720
OK, let's try the
monorepo approach.

00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:08.080
What would that look like?

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:10.240
The layout might be
something like this.

00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:15.340
Some files in the root, some
docs folder and test folder.

00:08:15.340 --> 00:08:16.520
We have the monolith.

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:20.830
They are moving from the
monolith to microservices.

00:08:20.830 --> 00:08:23.950
The monolith is still there, so
microservices are still there.

00:08:23.950 --> 00:08:27.220
One director for the monolith,
one services directory

00:08:27.220 --> 00:08:29.050
with all the different
microservices,

00:08:29.050 --> 00:08:31.120
have their own subdirectories.

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:33.130
They want to set
up this for growth.

00:08:33.130 --> 00:08:35.169
Right now, they have
five microservices.

00:08:35.169 --> 00:08:38.340
They will have
more in the future.

00:08:38.340 --> 00:08:42.150
Who reviews code when
you check code in?

00:08:42.150 --> 00:08:45.720
Well, James has found a
cool feature in GitLab,

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:48.270
and it's in some
other tools, as well,

00:08:48.270 --> 00:08:51.150
where you can add a
code owner's file.

00:08:51.150 --> 00:08:53.940
That code owner's
file tells the system

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:58.920
who should be the auto-assigned
reviewers for any code that's

00:08:58.920 --> 00:08:59.880
checked in.

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:02.040
And it also documents ownership.

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:03.870
If you're just
browsing the code you

00:09:03.870 --> 00:09:08.620
know who to ask about a
particular piece of code.

00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:09.120
Good.

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:11.280
They've decided to
go with monorepo.

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:14.400
First decision made,
one out of six done.

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:18.530
And it's time to
move on to number 2.

00:09:18.530 --> 00:09:21.010
ADAM ROSS: So next, we
have continuous testing,

00:09:21.010 --> 00:09:23.890
getting that test automation
going on an ongoing basis

00:09:23.890 --> 00:09:26.890
so that every change is
carefully measured and found

00:09:26.890 --> 00:09:29.770
to be acceptable
to move forward.

00:09:29.770 --> 00:09:32.020
So junior developers
might commonly

00:09:32.020 --> 00:09:33.940
think, oh, I can just
run the tests locally.

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:36.050
Everything will
work out just fine.

00:09:36.050 --> 00:09:37.930
But more senior
engineers know that can

00:09:37.930 --> 00:09:40.990
lead to inconsistently
running the tests and a lot

00:09:40.990 --> 00:09:43.720
of difficulty making sure that
you're testing things the way

00:09:43.720 --> 00:09:45.120
they operate in production.

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:47.530
Running end-to-end
tests on cloud services

00:09:47.530 --> 00:09:51.420
can be a little
tricky on your laptop.

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:53.270
So they're going to
take their existing

00:09:53.270 --> 00:09:55.070
work on building
their testing pipeline

00:09:55.070 --> 00:09:56.890
and extend it to
these new serverless

00:09:56.890 --> 00:09:58.950
services they've got.

00:09:58.950 --> 00:10:00.890
And maybe some of those
automation engineers

00:10:00.890 --> 00:10:02.682
amongst you would find
that fun, like Pari.

00:10:02.682 --> 00:10:04.932
MARTIN OMANDER: It does sound
kind of fun, doesn't it?

00:10:04.932 --> 00:10:06.400
I would want to build this.

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:07.220
ADAM ROSS: Yeah.

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:12.260
So here, we have the master
branch from the repo.

00:10:12.260 --> 00:10:13.880
The master branch
is commonly used

00:10:13.880 --> 00:10:17.390
as a release ready branch to
which all changes are merged.

00:10:17.390 --> 00:10:21.860
Now, shortly after the last
release, a bug fix was begun.

00:10:21.860 --> 00:10:23.780
This branch has been in
progress for a while,

00:10:23.780 --> 00:10:27.500
but in the interim, someone else
came along, fixed a problem,

00:10:27.500 --> 00:10:29.660
and merged that to master.

00:10:29.660 --> 00:10:32.390
So when we look at
this bug fix branch--

00:10:32.390 --> 00:10:34.490
ready to go, unit test passing.

00:10:34.490 --> 00:10:36.770
James is excited to merge it.

00:10:36.770 --> 00:10:38.600
Tess knows, wait a second.

00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:41.660
Even though that test is
passing, it's showing green.

00:10:41.660 --> 00:10:43.670
There is a logical
conflict between what

00:10:43.670 --> 00:10:47.880
is now in master and the change
that James has been working on.

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:49.970
Thank goodness there
was an engineer on hand

00:10:49.970 --> 00:10:52.760
that has memorized all
the code changes that

00:10:52.760 --> 00:10:55.650
have gone on for the
last number of months.

00:10:55.650 --> 00:10:59.210
So maybe the solution here
is to have trustworthy tests.

00:10:59.210 --> 00:11:02.540
If your automated tests
aren't reliable pretty much

00:11:02.540 --> 00:11:04.880
all the time, they're
not worth that much

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:07.910
because you learn to disregard
the results of running

00:11:07.910 --> 00:11:09.910
those tests.

00:11:09.910 --> 00:11:14.090
And so instead, we want to look
into continuous integration.

00:11:14.090 --> 00:11:17.870
Continuous integration takes
this continuous testing idea

00:11:17.870 --> 00:11:20.720
and focuses on how to make
sure that if you accepted

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:23.510
that change into your
main line of code,

00:11:23.510 --> 00:11:25.550
would things still be passing?

00:11:25.550 --> 00:11:28.850
This is done by merging
in the master branch

00:11:28.850 --> 00:11:32.090
here into the bug fixed branch
before running those tests,

00:11:32.090 --> 00:11:33.830
performing a code integration.

00:11:33.830 --> 00:11:36.650
Not to be confused with
an integration test, which

00:11:36.650 --> 00:11:38.055
is a bit of a different beast.

00:11:42.790 --> 00:11:45.410
And as we explored, we're
needing to build that pipeline.

00:11:45.410 --> 00:11:47.130
What kind of tools
should we use?

00:11:47.130 --> 00:11:49.430
Well, one of the most
important features

00:11:49.430 --> 00:11:53.000
that you might have
for your CI/CD system

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:55.340
would be a configuration
as code approach.

00:11:55.340 --> 00:11:57.290
Having the definition
of those jobs

00:11:57.290 --> 00:12:00.890
and all the operations committed
to your code repositories,

00:12:00.890 --> 00:12:04.190
so that the entire development
team and any ops engineers,

00:12:04.190 --> 00:12:06.800
and anyone else around that
technical neighborhood,

00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:11.030
can see those changes and weigh
in on what they constitute

00:12:11.030 --> 00:12:12.770
and make sure that
you're practicing

00:12:12.770 --> 00:12:15.095
a good and integrated
DevOps sort of culture.

00:12:17.830 --> 00:12:21.070
Now, as we've mentioned,
this team is using GitLab,

00:12:21.070 --> 00:12:22.570
and they're going
to continue to use

00:12:22.570 --> 00:12:24.290
that for these new services.

00:12:24.290 --> 00:12:26.440
But in the future, maybe
they'll outgrow GitLab.

00:12:26.440 --> 00:12:28.570
Maybe there'll be some
other considerations,

00:12:28.570 --> 00:12:30.070
and they'll consider
other products,

00:12:30.070 --> 00:12:33.610
such as CircleCI, Travis,
Jenkins, or Cloud Build, which

00:12:33.610 --> 00:12:37.570
is a product in GCP.

00:12:37.570 --> 00:12:41.200
A simple GitLab testing
pipeline is made of YAML.

00:12:41.200 --> 00:12:43.780
I bet a lot of you here were
thinking, oh, serverless track.

00:12:43.780 --> 00:12:47.470
We're safe from
YAML, but not always.

00:12:47.470 --> 00:12:52.870
So here, we have a stage
that includes a test stage.

00:12:52.870 --> 00:12:55.340
The only one for now, but
more will be added later.

00:12:55.340 --> 00:12:59.020
And every single job in a
test stage runs in parallel.

00:12:59.020 --> 00:13:01.420
And our initial jobs are
linting and unit testing.

00:13:01.420 --> 00:13:03.790
Linting is checking
your code syntax

00:13:03.790 --> 00:13:07.670
and unit tests are relatively
quick to run tests.

00:13:07.670 --> 00:13:10.270
But we see that both of
these are running node.

00:13:10.270 --> 00:13:12.595
They're both running
some implied service

00:13:12.595 --> 00:13:13.720
that they're testing again.

00:13:13.720 --> 00:13:17.560
So it's not necessarily a
good fit for a monorepo.

00:13:17.560 --> 00:13:20.930
It's not testing a bunch
of different services.

00:13:20.930 --> 00:13:23.020
So let's expand on
that linting job.

00:13:23.020 --> 00:13:25.250
Now we have two linting jobs.

00:13:25.250 --> 00:13:29.140
One is testing a node
service named relay,

00:13:29.140 --> 00:13:31.660
running the job inside
the really subdirectory

00:13:31.660 --> 00:13:33.310
of the repo, and
the other is linting

00:13:33.310 --> 00:13:38.590
a service built in Go running
inside a payment subdirectory.

00:13:38.590 --> 00:13:42.670
And as James is observing,
this multiplication of the jobs

00:13:42.670 --> 00:13:44.340
is going to result
in some slowdown

00:13:44.340 --> 00:13:48.960
as more and more services
are added to the repo.

00:13:48.960 --> 00:13:51.940
And so making the pipeline
a bit more dynamic

00:13:51.940 --> 00:13:54.580
is going to help that process.

00:13:54.580 --> 00:13:57.280
Using almost any
CI/CD system, you

00:13:57.280 --> 00:13:59.320
can introduce a
little dynamic logic

00:13:59.320 --> 00:14:01.600
and take advantage of the
fact that you're probably

00:14:01.600 --> 00:14:03.220
using version control.

00:14:03.220 --> 00:14:05.850
And you can run slightly
complex commands,

00:14:05.850 --> 00:14:08.500
such as this, this
git diff here,

00:14:08.500 --> 00:14:10.410
to list all the files
that have changed.

00:14:10.410 --> 00:14:14.350
Maybe only run the tests
that apply to those files.

00:14:14.350 --> 00:14:18.280
And as it happens, GitLab
has a trick for that.

00:14:18.280 --> 00:14:22.930
In the configuration, if you are
running your testing pipeline

00:14:22.930 --> 00:14:25.300
for a merge request,
you can also

00:14:25.300 --> 00:14:29.050
specify to only run that
job if a file changes

00:14:29.050 --> 00:14:31.190
in a particular subdirectory.

00:14:31.190 --> 00:14:34.840
So here, I see it is
configured to only run

00:14:34.840 --> 00:14:38.412
if a file and the relay service
has some kind of update.

00:14:38.412 --> 00:14:40.120
And you might be
thinking this is getting

00:14:40.120 --> 00:14:43.450
a little complicated, but
that's a trade-off from going

00:14:43.450 --> 00:14:44.470
with a monorepo.

00:14:44.470 --> 00:14:47.170
The tooling is often a
little behind on that

00:14:47.170 --> 00:14:50.260
compared to having a
service per repository.

00:14:53.010 --> 00:14:56.940
Lastly, with the monorepo
and having all these services

00:14:56.940 --> 00:14:59.730
in one repository, there
might be a tendency

00:14:59.730 --> 00:15:03.120
to have some of the operations
leak into top level directories

00:15:03.120 --> 00:15:06.040
or into this configuration file.

00:15:06.040 --> 00:15:09.360
Well, having those operations
defined in the world's

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:12.690
best known task
runner, bash scripts,

00:15:12.690 --> 00:15:16.110
you can get them isolated
into those same subdirectories

00:15:16.110 --> 00:15:20.910
and make sure that those
responsible for an individual

00:15:20.910 --> 00:15:22.890
service are also
responsible for all

00:15:22.890 --> 00:15:24.990
the details of the operations.

00:15:24.990 --> 00:15:27.720
And perhaps in the future,
this Into the Woods dev team

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:31.230
will look into to something
else that's a little bit more

00:15:31.230 --> 00:15:33.960
sophisticated than bash,
maybe using make files

00:15:33.960 --> 00:15:38.300
or Basil to run their jobs.

00:15:38.300 --> 00:15:41.960
And so by the time all of
these steps are built in,

00:15:41.960 --> 00:15:45.590
we have a few different stages
potentially coming together

00:15:45.590 --> 00:15:48.230
on just the testing pipeline.

00:15:48.230 --> 00:15:50.540
A pre-check here
is showing the use

00:15:50.540 --> 00:15:52.910
of shellcheck, which is
a utility that applies

00:15:52.910 --> 00:15:55.340
linting checks to bash scripts.

00:15:55.340 --> 00:15:58.250
Then test, running
linting and unit tests

00:15:58.250 --> 00:15:59.510
against each of the services.

00:15:59.510 --> 00:16:02.750
And build, running any builds
for those services that

00:16:02.750 --> 00:16:05.900
need a build process.

00:16:05.900 --> 00:16:09.020
MARTIN OMANDER: All right, so
that was the end of step 2.

00:16:09.020 --> 00:16:12.110
Now we're two down, four to go.

00:16:12.110 --> 00:16:14.990
The next decision the team of
Into the Woods need to make

00:16:14.990 --> 00:16:18.140
is how to manage
automatic deployment.

00:16:18.140 --> 00:16:20.840
So there are a number of ways
to do deployments, all the way

00:16:20.840 --> 00:16:24.320
from how CTO Sang did it before
he was a CTO, when he worked

00:16:24.320 --> 00:16:27.140
in a startup 20 years
ago, through something

00:16:27.140 --> 00:16:30.020
that a junior developer might
do with scripted development.

00:16:30.020 --> 00:16:32.120
The team really,
though, they really

00:16:32.120 --> 00:16:35.450
want to focus on continuous
deployment and delivery, rather

00:16:35.450 --> 00:16:38.090
than these sort of
semi-manual approaches,

00:16:38.090 --> 00:16:42.620
because that leads to the
more reproducible deployments

00:16:42.620 --> 00:16:45.020
and higher velocity.

00:16:45.020 --> 00:16:48.170
So James, he is a
happy junior developer.

00:16:48.170 --> 00:16:49.850
He checks in a bunch of code.

00:16:49.850 --> 00:16:51.440
It tests OK.

00:16:51.440 --> 00:16:53.450
He merges them.

00:16:53.450 --> 00:16:55.880
Ready to go on
the release train.

00:16:55.880 --> 00:16:58.400
Let's deploy it and production.

00:16:58.400 --> 00:16:59.970
Tess says, hold on a minute.

00:16:59.970 --> 00:17:03.410
How do we know that
this won't break?

00:17:03.410 --> 00:17:04.940
Pari has a solution.

00:17:04.940 --> 00:17:08.660
We deploy it to staging, and
we deploy it all the time

00:17:08.660 --> 00:17:11.839
to staging and keep
running tests there.

00:17:11.839 --> 00:17:14.960
And that way, we can see if
it would break if we were

00:17:14.960 --> 00:17:16.540
to deploy it to production.

00:17:16.540 --> 00:17:18.040
Let's see what that
would look like.

00:17:20.790 --> 00:17:24.800
ADAM ROSS: So adding deployment
to this whole pipeline

00:17:24.800 --> 00:17:27.260
is really just adding
an additional stage

00:17:27.260 --> 00:17:29.150
to the end of what
we've already built,

00:17:29.150 --> 00:17:33.510
and that stage will include
some new deployment jobs.

00:17:33.510 --> 00:17:37.500
These jobs bring with it
more YAML for us to look at.

00:17:37.500 --> 00:17:42.040
A deployed job definitely needs
to run the deploy process,

00:17:42.040 --> 00:17:45.690
and that will probably involve
using G Cloud, the command line

00:17:45.690 --> 00:17:49.500
SDK that allows you to run
operations against GCP.

00:17:49.500 --> 00:17:53.160
So we have a shorthand
authentication

00:17:53.160 --> 00:17:55.160
for G Cloud to the
Cloud Platform,

00:17:55.160 --> 00:17:57.000
and we'll go into more
detail on that later

00:17:57.000 --> 00:17:59.310
in the security
section of the talk.

00:17:59.310 --> 00:18:02.430
And then under the script,
we're changing directory

00:18:02.430 --> 00:18:05.580
into the relay service, and
we're running our functions

00:18:05.580 --> 00:18:08.100
deploy for that
Cloud Function, which

00:18:08.100 --> 00:18:10.440
probably should be wrapped
in a deploy script itself.

00:18:10.440 --> 00:18:13.350
But it's more clear this
way that that is not

00:18:13.350 --> 00:18:16.020
a very complicated
script, necessarily.

00:18:16.020 --> 00:18:18.990
Now, if you were going to
deploy instead a cloud run

00:18:18.990 --> 00:18:20.700
service, which you
may have heard today

00:18:20.700 --> 00:18:24.240
across the keynote
or other sessions,

00:18:24.240 --> 00:18:26.610
you know that you're dealing
with serverless containers.

00:18:26.610 --> 00:18:29.070
They have a bit of a
different deployment model.

00:18:29.070 --> 00:18:32.760
And so you are deploying
a container image

00:18:32.760 --> 00:18:37.380
from Google Container Registry
to your cloud run service.

00:18:37.380 --> 00:18:39.990
But it is just the same,
a one-line command,

00:18:39.990 --> 00:18:40.920
to make it happen.

00:18:43.940 --> 00:18:46.570
So we have added the
new deployment jobs

00:18:46.570 --> 00:18:48.100
to the end of our pipeline.

00:18:48.100 --> 00:18:49.720
And so on every
change, it will run

00:18:49.720 --> 00:18:51.550
through each of these
stages, ultimately

00:18:51.550 --> 00:18:54.370
resulting in deployed code.

00:18:54.370 --> 00:18:58.540
MARTIN OMANDER: All right,
three down, three to go.

00:18:58.540 --> 00:19:00.790
The next step is environments.

00:19:00.790 --> 00:19:04.210
How do we deal with
that at Into the Woods?

00:19:04.210 --> 00:19:06.640
So just a quick reminder
what environments are.

00:19:06.640 --> 00:19:08.420
We have the architecture.

00:19:08.420 --> 00:19:12.550
That's the design, what computer
is in microservice, what

00:19:12.550 --> 00:19:14.880
storage is in a microservice.

00:19:14.880 --> 00:19:16.810
Environments-- those
are instantiations

00:19:16.810 --> 00:19:18.520
of that architecture,
and we have them

00:19:18.520 --> 00:19:22.450
for various uses, like
demo, and test, and so on.

00:19:22.450 --> 00:19:26.000
All right, so the team
sits down to discuss this.

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:29.620
Pari says, let's
minimize administration

00:19:29.620 --> 00:19:35.260
by having all these environments
live in one GCP project.

00:19:35.260 --> 00:19:36.340
James is like, no.

00:19:36.340 --> 00:19:38.380
Let's have them more isolated.

00:19:38.380 --> 00:19:41.230
Let's have one environment
per GCP project.

00:19:41.230 --> 00:19:44.290
We'll have many,
many, many projects.

00:19:44.290 --> 00:19:49.310
Well, we need to look into the
pros and cons of each approach.

00:19:49.310 --> 00:19:52.090
So if each environment
is a namespace,

00:19:52.090 --> 00:19:57.370
here we have QA stage for
the payments microservice.

00:19:57.370 --> 00:20:00.790
We have a Payments Compute
part and Payments Data

00:20:00.790 --> 00:20:05.750
part for QA and for stage.

00:20:05.750 --> 00:20:07.900
So only one project--

00:20:07.900 --> 00:20:09.840
it has certain advantages.

00:20:09.840 --> 00:20:11.580
Pari is right about that.

00:20:11.580 --> 00:20:13.270
There is easy access management.

00:20:13.270 --> 00:20:17.110
You go into IAM at
once and set that up.

00:20:17.110 --> 00:20:20.830
It is also easy to just list
all your resources in a project,

00:20:20.830 --> 00:20:24.050
because it's all right
there in one project.

00:20:24.050 --> 00:20:28.060
However, there are some
global projects settings

00:20:28.060 --> 00:20:31.390
that you might have seen
around regions and data,

00:20:31.390 --> 00:20:33.910
App Engine data
store, and so on,

00:20:33.910 --> 00:20:36.940
that we need set
once per project.

00:20:36.940 --> 00:20:44.050
And that could be hard to
make all the environments use

00:20:44.050 --> 00:20:46.300
those global settings.

00:20:46.300 --> 00:20:51.550
Also, it's extra work to
connect the compute and the data

00:20:51.550 --> 00:20:53.320
for these.

00:20:53.320 --> 00:20:57.310
As a matter of fact, it could
get so bad that you crosswire

00:20:57.310 --> 00:20:59.960
and there's crosstalk
between environments.

00:20:59.960 --> 00:21:03.790
So the team decides not to have
everything in one giant GCP

00:21:03.790 --> 00:21:06.140
project.

00:21:06.140 --> 00:21:09.310
Well, the alternative
is every environment

00:21:09.310 --> 00:21:11.890
is a separate GCP project.

00:21:11.890 --> 00:21:17.800
So we have a staging project,
and we have a QA project.

00:21:17.800 --> 00:21:20.390
So the disadvantage
of this is there

00:21:20.390 --> 00:21:21.980
is more management, of course.

00:21:21.980 --> 00:21:28.520
We need to set up user access
for every single project.

00:21:28.520 --> 00:21:32.450
Another one is we have to keep
track of all these projects.

00:21:32.450 --> 00:21:34.150
What's good about
this, though, is

00:21:34.150 --> 00:21:38.230
that we have really strong
isolation with no crosstalk

00:21:38.230 --> 00:21:40.310
between environments.

00:21:40.310 --> 00:21:43.400
So the team decides to
go with this approach.

00:21:43.400 --> 00:21:46.660
Now, the question is, how
do we avoid QA bottlenecks?

00:21:46.660 --> 00:21:50.200
In the old pre-serverless
days in Into the Woods,

00:21:50.200 --> 00:21:52.383
there was one QA environment.

00:21:52.383 --> 00:21:53.800
And if you're
working on a branch,

00:21:53.800 --> 00:21:55.840
you really wanted to
test your stuff on that,

00:21:55.840 --> 00:21:58.960
and everybody was fighting
to get their stuff on there.

00:21:58.960 --> 00:22:01.360
Well, new environments--
everything is serverless.

00:22:01.360 --> 00:22:05.860
We can spin up as many
environments as we want, right?

00:22:05.860 --> 00:22:08.030
Well, we do need to
keep track of them.

00:22:08.030 --> 00:22:10.210
So maybe we should
give each developer

00:22:10.210 --> 00:22:13.130
their own development
environment and their own QA

00:22:13.130 --> 00:22:16.100
environment, rather than
infinite environments.

00:22:16.100 --> 00:22:18.880
Let's see what that
would look like.

00:22:18.880 --> 00:22:21.510
ADAM ROSS: So now that we
have a plan around the number

00:22:21.510 --> 00:22:25.020
of environments, we have
production, staging, and then

00:22:25.020 --> 00:22:27.268
a QA environment
for each developer,

00:22:27.268 --> 00:22:29.310
and a development environment
for each developer,

00:22:29.310 --> 00:22:31.020
so that they can experiment.

00:22:31.020 --> 00:22:32.700
And each of these
environments is

00:22:32.700 --> 00:22:36.150
related to the code
changes in some way.

00:22:36.150 --> 00:22:39.240
Production is going to be tagged
off of the master branch when

00:22:39.240 --> 00:22:40.710
releases are cut.

00:22:40.710 --> 00:22:43.290
And master branch is going
to automatically deploy

00:22:43.290 --> 00:22:44.280
the staging.

00:22:44.280 --> 00:22:49.470
And then individual feature
branches will deploy to QA.

00:22:49.470 --> 00:22:52.530
One of the challenges with
this arrangement of associating

00:22:52.530 --> 00:22:54.360
different pieces,
different lines

00:22:54.360 --> 00:22:56.010
of code to different
environments,

00:22:56.010 --> 00:22:59.910
is how the CI/CD system
understands how to route that.

00:22:59.910 --> 00:23:04.550
And for that, we have
a bit of bash magic.

00:23:04.550 --> 00:23:10.140
Here, if the branch is master,
this first part of the code

00:23:10.140 --> 00:23:13.380
says, oh, this
must be for stage.

00:23:13.380 --> 00:23:16.460
And after the else
statement, we are looking up

00:23:16.460 --> 00:23:20.270
a variable from the environment
to associate the developer that

00:23:20.270 --> 00:23:23.450
pushed that last
change, and that

00:23:23.450 --> 00:23:26.090
will map to that
developer's environment.

00:23:26.090 --> 00:23:28.820
Now, you could hardwire
that and say, oh, we're

00:23:28.820 --> 00:23:31.600
going to just use the
test environment today.

00:23:31.600 --> 00:23:34.290
But by providing that
little bit of separation,

00:23:34.290 --> 00:23:35.870
it provides space
to then continue

00:23:35.870 --> 00:23:39.500
to expand this later to perhaps
have multiple environments that

00:23:39.500 --> 00:23:40.430
could be routed to.

00:23:44.080 --> 00:23:48.920
And so using this
arrangement, our earlier fix

00:23:48.920 --> 00:23:51.500
to the master branch is
going to automatically deploy

00:23:51.500 --> 00:23:53.060
to the staging environment.

00:23:53.060 --> 00:23:54.950
And bug fix number
1, as it's going

00:23:54.950 --> 00:23:56.750
through its
troubleshooting cycles,

00:23:56.750 --> 00:23:59.850
will be deployed to
James's QA environment.

00:23:59.850 --> 00:24:03.050
Once that is approved to
be merged into master,

00:24:03.050 --> 00:24:05.420
it, too, will be
automatically deployed,

00:24:05.420 --> 00:24:08.448
updating the staging environment
to the current picture of what

00:24:08.448 --> 00:24:10.490
would happen if a production
release were tagged.

00:24:14.540 --> 00:24:16.970
And of course, once
a release is tagged,

00:24:16.970 --> 00:24:20.020
production will be deployed.

00:24:20.020 --> 00:24:22.570
MARTIN OMANDER:
All right, the team

00:24:22.570 --> 00:24:26.480
has decided on four out
of six decision points.

00:24:26.480 --> 00:24:29.793
The next one is
serverless testing.

00:24:29.793 --> 00:24:31.960
Now, this is a little
different than regular testing

00:24:31.960 --> 00:24:34.930
if you're testing something
you run in your own data center

00:24:34.930 --> 00:24:36.470
or on-prem.

00:24:36.470 --> 00:24:39.130
Serverless is a little
bit of a different beast.

00:24:39.130 --> 00:24:42.460
So there are, of course,
different types of testing.

00:24:42.460 --> 00:24:45.100
Junior developers
think of units testing.

00:24:45.100 --> 00:24:48.010
There's integration
testing, system testing.

00:24:48.010 --> 00:24:50.140
Sang, the CTO, of
course, he thinks

00:24:50.140 --> 00:24:52.060
more about end to end testing.

00:24:52.060 --> 00:24:54.430
So we have this whole
spectrum of tests.

00:24:54.430 --> 00:24:56.050
One isn't better than the other.

00:24:56.050 --> 00:24:58.030
Some are really
useful to developers,

00:24:58.030 --> 00:25:00.100
some are more useful
to business people.

00:25:00.100 --> 00:25:03.980
We need a mix of all these
different types of testing.

00:25:03.980 --> 00:25:05.690
How do we do that?

00:25:05.690 --> 00:25:08.110
Well, so here, again,
we have bug fix 1,

00:25:08.110 --> 00:25:10.220
the branch James has created.

00:25:10.220 --> 00:25:12.020
He needs to merge this in.

00:25:12.020 --> 00:25:18.010
Let's see what testing happens
at each place in this scenario.

00:25:18.010 --> 00:25:19.720
So whenever he commits code--

00:25:19.720 --> 00:25:21.070
that's the heart.

00:25:21.070 --> 00:25:23.740
James loves his
code, it's a heart--

00:25:23.740 --> 00:25:28.370
there is unit testing on
linting in the CI workspace.

00:25:28.370 --> 00:25:30.370
So this still has nothing
to do with serverless,

00:25:30.370 --> 00:25:33.580
or nothing to do with Cloud
Functions, or Cloud Run,

00:25:33.580 --> 00:25:35.110
or anything like that.

00:25:35.110 --> 00:25:36.910
It's auto-deployed, as we saw.

00:25:36.910 --> 00:25:39.330
It's auto-deployed to james-qa.

00:25:39.330 --> 00:25:42.310
This is James's
own QA environment.

00:25:42.310 --> 00:25:44.400
And when it's
auto-deployed there,

00:25:44.400 --> 00:25:47.320
the automatic system
tests are running there.

00:25:47.320 --> 00:25:52.180
They run in the context of
serverless, so this run on GCP.

00:25:52.180 --> 00:25:55.330
And you can use all the
GCP services and databases

00:25:55.330 --> 00:25:56.120
and such.

00:25:56.120 --> 00:25:58.900
They are more bigger
than unit tests.

00:25:58.900 --> 00:26:01.600
Then when he merges
into master, there

00:26:01.600 --> 00:26:04.240
are lint tests in
the CI/CD system.

00:26:04.240 --> 00:26:06.590
And then it's all
deployed to staging.

00:26:06.590 --> 00:26:10.700
Their system tests run,
just like on james-qa.

00:26:10.700 --> 00:26:12.760
And there is also--

00:26:12.760 --> 00:26:16.300
these more business-focused
end-to-end tests

00:26:16.300 --> 00:26:18.340
are run in staging.

00:26:18.340 --> 00:26:20.020
And there, all
branches are mixed up

00:26:20.020 --> 00:26:24.190
in staging, too, so
we test all of them.

00:26:24.190 --> 00:26:27.280
So broadly speaking,
there are two types

00:26:27.280 --> 00:26:29.080
of testing environments here.

00:26:29.080 --> 00:26:32.590
Inside the CI/CD system,
that's good for simple testing,

00:26:32.590 --> 00:26:34.480
like unit tests and linting.

00:26:34.480 --> 00:26:36.340
But when you
actually test stuff,

00:26:36.340 --> 00:26:39.400
like server databases
and making calls,

00:26:39.400 --> 00:26:42.370
and you're exercising
more of GCP,

00:26:42.370 --> 00:26:45.040
then you actually need to
run those tests on GCP.

00:26:47.770 --> 00:26:50.200
So there are very
serverless options.

00:26:50.200 --> 00:26:53.980
You may know of serverless
functions, Cloud Functions.

00:26:53.980 --> 00:26:55.360
You may know of App Engine.

00:26:55.360 --> 00:26:58.700
And earlier today, you heard
about Cloud Functions--

00:26:58.700 --> 00:27:00.560
sorry, Cloud Run.

00:27:00.560 --> 00:27:02.500
Let's go through and
see how you would

00:27:02.500 --> 00:27:06.910
test each one of these three
serverless computer options.

00:27:06.910 --> 00:27:09.562
Testing Cloud Function,
you can do that locally.

00:27:09.562 --> 00:27:11.020
There are various
ways of doing it.

00:27:11.020 --> 00:27:13.480
We really recommend, if
you want to know more

00:27:13.480 --> 00:27:15.520
about testing Cloud
Functions locally,

00:27:15.520 --> 00:27:19.960
you should go to our
co-worker's, Stewart's talk,

00:27:19.960 --> 00:27:22.990
on Thursday afternoon,
where he will dig

00:27:22.990 --> 00:27:26.140
into more details around this.

00:27:26.140 --> 00:27:29.610
For unit testing, you would
create mock HTTP requests.

00:27:29.610 --> 00:27:32.230
And for system testing,
well, in that case,

00:27:32.230 --> 00:27:34.930
you just you deploy to GCP.

00:27:34.930 --> 00:27:40.180
You get the URL and then your
test system hits that URL.

00:27:40.180 --> 00:27:42.640
Also worth noting
here, Tess notes

00:27:42.640 --> 00:27:47.680
that it's good to have
more complex logic in sort

00:27:47.680 --> 00:27:50.800
of secondary functions that
aren't directly called.

00:27:50.800 --> 00:27:54.070
So that makes it easier
to just sort of test

00:27:54.070 --> 00:27:56.950
those and troubleshoot
those in a local environment

00:27:56.950 --> 00:28:00.800
before you deploy
everything up to GCP.

00:28:00.800 --> 00:28:04.100
On App Engine, with the
modern runtimes on App Engine,

00:28:04.100 --> 00:28:05.810
you do your local
development test,

00:28:05.810 --> 00:28:09.170
using your regular tools or your
regular frameworks, whatever

00:28:09.170 --> 00:28:12.690
you're using, Flask,
Express, et cetera.

00:28:12.690 --> 00:28:16.010
Unit testing, there are mock
libraries that mock out,

00:28:16.010 --> 00:28:18.830
like task, queues,
and databases,

00:28:18.830 --> 00:28:20.520
and things like that.

00:28:20.520 --> 00:28:23.750
And for system testing, it's
just like Cloud Functions.

00:28:23.750 --> 00:28:28.790
You deploy it to GCP, you get
a URL and your test system

00:28:28.790 --> 00:28:30.590
hits that URL.

00:28:30.590 --> 00:28:33.733
Also worth noting here is that
App Engine has been around

00:28:33.733 --> 00:28:34.400
for a long time.

00:28:34.400 --> 00:28:37.490
So there is lots and lots
of content, lots of articles

00:28:37.490 --> 00:28:39.680
and blog posts by
Googlers and non-Googlers

00:28:39.680 --> 00:28:43.430
about how to test it.

00:28:43.430 --> 00:28:46.560
That was two of the
serverless compute options.

00:28:46.560 --> 00:28:50.610
What about the third one that
we heard about earlier today?

00:28:50.610 --> 00:28:53.105
ADAM ROSS: Well, since
Cloud Run is new,

00:28:53.105 --> 00:28:54.480
let's talk a little
about what it

00:28:54.480 --> 00:28:57.210
is to set the groundwork
for how we would test in it.

00:28:57.210 --> 00:29:01.710
Cloud Run bridges the worlds
of serverless and containers,

00:29:01.710 --> 00:29:04.830
providing a way to run
a containerized service

00:29:04.830 --> 00:29:07.150
serverlessly.

00:29:07.150 --> 00:29:10.090
This means that you can
build a service in a pretty

00:29:10.090 --> 00:29:14.110
conventional way, wrap it in
a container, and deploy it.

00:29:14.110 --> 00:29:18.940
If you have no ops team, like
Tess's team seems to have,

00:29:18.940 --> 00:29:20.560
you can use managed Cloud Run.

00:29:20.560 --> 00:29:23.380
And otherwise, you can
have Cloud Run on GKE,

00:29:23.380 --> 00:29:25.150
where you have a
Kubernetes cluster

00:29:25.150 --> 00:29:28.120
underneath that your
own ops team can manage.

00:29:28.120 --> 00:29:31.360
And with this arrangement,
all the considerations

00:29:31.360 --> 00:29:35.680
of how you would test a
container come into play.

00:29:35.680 --> 00:29:37.300
For local development
and debugging,

00:29:37.300 --> 00:29:39.400
you might run your
service locally

00:29:39.400 --> 00:29:40.930
using your standard
tools, or you

00:29:40.930 --> 00:29:44.230
might choose to build that
container locally and run it

00:29:44.230 --> 00:29:46.690
so that you are more closely
simulating the production

00:29:46.690 --> 00:29:48.130
environment.

00:29:48.130 --> 00:29:51.100
For unit testing, you
could also do the same,

00:29:51.100 --> 00:29:53.230
run your unit tests the
way you normally would

00:29:53.230 --> 00:29:54.520
when building code locally.

00:29:54.520 --> 00:29:56.830
Or you might also
build a container

00:29:56.830 --> 00:30:00.070
that's loaded up with a number
of development dependencies

00:30:00.070 --> 00:30:03.220
on top of the same
production dependencies

00:30:03.220 --> 00:30:05.920
that you have on
Cloud Run itself.

00:30:05.920 --> 00:30:08.470
And in that case, you can
run the unit tests there

00:30:08.470 --> 00:30:10.120
and have available
any of the system

00:30:10.120 --> 00:30:12.190
packages or other
resources that are

00:30:12.190 --> 00:30:14.440
part of your service container.

00:30:14.440 --> 00:30:16.990
For system testing, much
as we were suggesting

00:30:16.990 --> 00:30:18.790
with Cloud Functions
and App Engine,

00:30:18.790 --> 00:30:21.630
go ahead and deploy your
service to Cloud Run.

00:30:21.630 --> 00:30:23.860
It will only be running
when you're testing it,

00:30:23.860 --> 00:30:27.265
and otherwise, it will scale to
0 and not cause you troubles.

00:30:32.440 --> 00:30:35.500
With all of the
different jobs that we've

00:30:35.500 --> 00:30:38.080
been looking at with
the CI/CD system,

00:30:38.080 --> 00:30:39.930
there is this workspace
that is spun up,

00:30:39.930 --> 00:30:41.680
in which we're running
the linting checks,

00:30:41.680 --> 00:30:46.090
running unit tests, and running
G Cloud to trigger deployments.

00:30:46.090 --> 00:30:47.860
And one of the
problems you might find

00:30:47.860 --> 00:30:49.690
are the performance
concerns of what

00:30:49.690 --> 00:30:51.490
it takes to initialize
that environment,

00:30:51.490 --> 00:30:52.960
then run all the tests.

00:30:52.960 --> 00:30:57.100
And waiting for tests
is kind of a bummer.

00:30:57.100 --> 00:31:00.790
So one thing we can do is
find ways to optimize just how

00:31:00.790 --> 00:31:02.260
big those workspaces are.

00:31:02.260 --> 00:31:04.630
Can we use a smaller
container image

00:31:04.630 --> 00:31:10.390
as part of that CI/CD workspace
to initialize the environment?

00:31:10.390 --> 00:31:13.270
Now, if the main
Cloud SDK Docker

00:31:13.270 --> 00:31:17.710
image that you might
use in a CI/CD workspace

00:31:17.710 --> 00:31:20.470
is pretty fully loaded--
it's based on Debian,

00:31:20.470 --> 00:31:22.420
it has a number of
emulators and other things

00:31:22.420 --> 00:31:25.970
that you might not need
initially in a service.

00:31:25.970 --> 00:31:29.440
So you might want to go with
a more stripped down approach,

00:31:29.440 --> 00:31:31.780
using an Alpine-based image--

00:31:31.780 --> 00:31:34.270
that's a lean
distribution of Linux

00:31:34.270 --> 00:31:36.400
that's really popular
in containers--

00:31:36.400 --> 00:31:39.470
and add to it just the pieces
you need, in this case,

00:31:39.470 --> 00:31:41.080
for your Cloud Run work.

00:31:43.600 --> 00:31:48.170
And so the team here has built
a slightly customized version

00:31:48.170 --> 00:31:53.090
of the Cloud SDK, pulling
a static binary from Docker

00:31:53.090 --> 00:31:55.580
for the Docker client,
and then adding that

00:31:55.580 --> 00:32:00.260
to the official Alpine version
of the Cloud SDK Docker image,

00:32:00.260 --> 00:32:02.330
and installing the
beta components, which

00:32:02.330 --> 00:32:06.240
you need in order to interact
with the Cloud Run service.

00:32:06.240 --> 00:32:09.440
This can lighten the size
of this workspace container

00:32:09.440 --> 00:32:13.760
from around 700 megabytes
to 120 megabytes,

00:32:13.760 --> 00:32:17.060
and that can save you a
good minute of download time

00:32:17.060 --> 00:32:18.125
on each of those jobs.

00:32:23.060 --> 00:32:26.320
Another problem that can emerge
from the particular way we've

00:32:26.320 --> 00:32:29.290
outlined the system
here is the tendency

00:32:29.290 --> 00:32:33.160
to only trigger the tests when
a developer takes an action.

00:32:33.160 --> 00:32:36.320
For continuously evolving
software, that seems fine.

00:32:36.320 --> 00:32:38.750
You'll trigger the tests
on an ongoing basis,

00:32:38.750 --> 00:32:41.150
and they'll continually
be exercised.

00:32:41.150 --> 00:32:43.750
But for a mature service
that has already been built

00:32:43.750 --> 00:32:45.520
and is running happily
in the background,

00:32:45.520 --> 00:32:50.110
without anyone the wiser, you
won't notice if bit rot sets in

00:32:50.110 --> 00:32:52.390
and makes that service
unstable if you

00:32:52.390 --> 00:32:55.540
were to try to deploy it
or try to issue changes.

00:32:55.540 --> 00:32:58.660
And so rerunning the
deployment and testing process

00:32:58.660 --> 00:33:00.850
on all your services
is a viable way

00:33:00.850 --> 00:33:03.850
to make sure that they are all
staying in a healthy state,

00:33:03.850 --> 00:33:08.660
whether or not developers
are actively working on them.

00:33:08.660 --> 00:33:10.840
MARTIN OMANDER: So with
this, they've made decisions

00:33:10.840 --> 00:33:13.910
in five out of the six areas.

00:33:13.910 --> 00:33:14.900
They're almost there.

00:33:14.900 --> 00:33:17.740
The CI/CD pipeline
is almost complete.

00:33:17.740 --> 00:33:20.950
There is one area that is
very important you should not

00:33:20.950 --> 00:33:24.350
forget about, of course--
that is security.

00:33:24.350 --> 00:33:26.550
So the team sits down
to discuss security.

00:33:26.550 --> 00:33:30.610
Pari notes that, for
her deployment scripts,

00:33:30.610 --> 00:33:35.890
they need some secrets
to deploy to GCP.

00:33:35.890 --> 00:33:40.270
Tess notes that are to hit the
payments API that the payment

00:33:40.270 --> 00:33:45.505
provider, she needs an API key.

00:33:45.505 --> 00:33:47.380
So here we see, right
away from the beginning

00:33:47.380 --> 00:33:50.580
of the discussion,
two types of secrets.

00:33:50.580 --> 00:33:53.110
We have operational
secrets, on one hand,

00:33:53.110 --> 00:33:57.160
that deal with just managing
all the bills and CI/CD,

00:33:57.160 --> 00:33:58.190
all that stuff.

00:33:58.190 --> 00:34:00.760
And then we have
runtime secrets that

00:34:00.760 --> 00:34:03.460
are used by the
runtime for when it

00:34:03.460 --> 00:34:05.830
does what it's supposed to do.

00:34:05.830 --> 00:34:07.580
At that point, James
comes in and says,

00:34:07.580 --> 00:34:09.760
well, we have secure
source control.

00:34:09.760 --> 00:34:11.650
This is not an open
source project.

00:34:11.650 --> 00:34:14.860
Just deploy it all
to source control.

00:34:14.860 --> 00:34:18.489
At this point, Pari has to
come in and gently guide

00:34:18.489 --> 00:34:22.190
James and say that this
is not something one does.

00:34:22.190 --> 00:34:23.949
There could be
accidental disclosure.

00:34:23.949 --> 00:34:27.790
I mean, we don't want
developers to see the passwords

00:34:27.790 --> 00:34:30.739
and then accidentally
enter it somewhere else.

00:34:30.739 --> 00:34:32.949
We also have contractors
working from time to time

00:34:32.949 --> 00:34:34.449
at Into the Woods.

00:34:34.449 --> 00:34:37.719
Also, sometimes we share
code with other companies.

00:34:37.719 --> 00:34:39.670
We could even have
people potentially break

00:34:39.670 --> 00:34:41.889
into our source control system.

00:34:41.889 --> 00:34:44.050
These secrets need
to be in a safer

00:34:44.050 --> 00:34:46.270
place than source control.

00:34:46.270 --> 00:34:48.699
How do we do this?

00:34:48.699 --> 00:34:51.170
Pari has a proposal.

00:34:51.170 --> 00:34:55.330
So let's manage the
operational secrets separately

00:34:55.330 --> 00:34:58.000
from the runtime secrets.

00:34:58.000 --> 00:35:00.460
So everything starts
with the repo, right?

00:35:00.460 --> 00:35:02.700
We have the source code there.

00:35:02.700 --> 00:35:06.730
Now, James, no secrets
in the source code.

00:35:06.730 --> 00:35:11.260
Then that code gets pulled
in by the CI/CD system,

00:35:11.260 --> 00:35:16.390
and the CI/CD system has the
operational secrets in it.

00:35:16.390 --> 00:35:20.180
One operational secret is the
service key, service account

00:35:20.180 --> 00:35:20.680
key.

00:35:20.680 --> 00:35:25.320
That is used to
deploy code to GCP.

00:35:25.320 --> 00:35:28.620
The other operational
secret is the name

00:35:28.620 --> 00:35:33.360
of a storage bucket, that
Google Cloud Storage bucket.

00:35:33.360 --> 00:35:37.320
And that is deployed, or set,
as an environment variable

00:35:37.320 --> 00:35:40.560
in the runtime environment so
the runtime environment can

00:35:40.560 --> 00:35:42.275
read it.

00:35:42.275 --> 00:35:43.650
What does the
runtime environment

00:35:43.650 --> 00:35:46.750
do with the name of
the storage bucket?

00:35:46.750 --> 00:35:50.370
Well, when it needs a
secret, like the API

00:35:50.370 --> 00:35:53.160
key for the payment API,
it can go and hit that,

00:35:53.160 --> 00:35:56.190
read from that Cloud
Storage bucket,

00:35:56.190 --> 00:35:58.970
and get that runtime secret.

00:35:58.970 --> 00:36:02.220
And what's cool about this
is that now that reading

00:36:02.220 --> 00:36:09.240
of the runtime secret is managed
through IAM in GCP, which

00:36:09.240 --> 00:36:11.940
means it can be really
locked down, who can read it.

00:36:11.940 --> 00:36:13.900
James can't read
it, for example.

00:36:13.900 --> 00:36:14.850
Pari can't read it.

00:36:14.850 --> 00:36:16.950
Maybe none of the
developers can read it.

00:36:16.950 --> 00:36:22.320
Maybe it's only the CTO,
Sang, who can read it,

00:36:22.320 --> 00:36:24.660
and the runtime environment.

00:36:24.660 --> 00:36:29.190
So setting up this environment
means operational secrets,

00:36:29.190 --> 00:36:32.410
runtime secrets separately.

00:36:32.410 --> 00:36:34.350
Let's talk about how
those operational secrets

00:36:34.350 --> 00:36:37.690
are put into the CI/CD system.

00:36:37.690 --> 00:36:39.460
In GitLab, it's easy.

00:36:39.460 --> 00:36:42.490
You just enter
them in the web UI.

00:36:42.490 --> 00:36:44.880
This means there is
no file sitting around

00:36:44.880 --> 00:36:46.080
with these secrets.

00:36:46.080 --> 00:36:49.410
As soon as you have a
file, it's very dangerous

00:36:49.410 --> 00:36:52.650
because somebody might
accidentally check it in.

00:36:52.650 --> 00:36:56.160
In this way, somebody
types them into the web UI.

00:36:56.160 --> 00:36:58.860
Nobody saves them anywhere.

00:36:58.860 --> 00:37:03.363
Once you type these in,
let's see where they go next.

00:37:03.363 --> 00:37:05.530
ADAM ROSS: So if we look
at the particular variables

00:37:05.530 --> 00:37:09.080
we have here, the third one
down is the service account key.

00:37:09.080 --> 00:37:13.420
This is a key associated with
a service account from IAM.

00:37:13.420 --> 00:37:17.110
And it's been entered here
in a base64 encoded way.

00:37:17.110 --> 00:37:19.510
That way, if anyone
accidentally looks at it,

00:37:19.510 --> 00:37:22.210
takes a screenshot, echoes
it goes out to terminally,

00:37:22.210 --> 00:37:24.670
they have not
accidentally seen it.

00:37:24.670 --> 00:37:27.310
It's a nice security
through obscurity.

00:37:27.310 --> 00:37:31.750
And so using that, every single
job that runs in the CI/CD

00:37:31.750 --> 00:37:36.400
system can then
expand on that key,

00:37:36.400 --> 00:37:38.200
taking that
environment variable,

00:37:38.200 --> 00:37:41.070
decoding it, and then using
that to authenticate G

00:37:41.070 --> 00:37:43.600
cloud so that it
can go on, pretend

00:37:43.600 --> 00:37:46.430
to be that service account
as it goes through and runs

00:37:46.430 --> 00:37:49.270
any deployment or other
commands that you need to run.

00:37:52.670 --> 00:37:55.940
Now, as Pari notes here,
you might find that this

00:37:55.940 --> 00:37:58.825
is a little bit easier if you
were running from Cloud Build,

00:37:58.825 --> 00:38:00.200
because with Cloud
Build, instead

00:38:00.200 --> 00:38:02.900
of needing to manage
this key separately,

00:38:02.900 --> 00:38:05.690
you can instead grant
permissions to the Cloud

00:38:05.690 --> 00:38:09.080
Service account directly and
it will operate with that,

00:38:09.080 --> 00:38:12.350
no additional keys needed.

00:38:12.350 --> 00:38:14.570
So once you have
this service account,

00:38:14.570 --> 00:38:15.980
what exactly do
you do to give it

00:38:15.980 --> 00:38:19.160
those accesses, all
the permissions that it

00:38:19.160 --> 00:38:21.140
needs to run builds
and deployments

00:38:21.140 --> 00:38:23.060
and perform other operations?

00:38:23.060 --> 00:38:25.220
You could give it the
project owner or editor role.

00:38:25.220 --> 00:38:27.090
That's the easy
button way to do it.

00:38:27.090 --> 00:38:30.380
But in that case, if anything
were to expose that key,

00:38:30.380 --> 00:38:33.650
the blast radius of that
would be encompassing almost

00:38:33.650 --> 00:38:35.948
all the things that can be
done with your GCP project.

00:38:35.948 --> 00:38:37.490
MARTIN OMANDER:
Yeah, some could just

00:38:37.490 --> 00:38:39.670
take down the production
environment with that key,

00:38:39.670 --> 00:38:40.010
right?

00:38:40.010 --> 00:38:40.790
ADAM ROSS: It could
just delete it.

00:38:40.790 --> 00:38:42.240
MARTIN OMANDER: Delete it, ew.

00:38:42.240 --> 00:38:44.780
ADAM ROSS: So Instead, a
least privilege approach

00:38:44.780 --> 00:38:46.250
is much better.

00:38:46.250 --> 00:38:50.060
Figure out exactly which
roles and Cloud Storage bucket

00:38:50.060 --> 00:38:54.320
ACLs you need to have and assign
them to the service account.

00:38:54.320 --> 00:38:57.200
That way, you know that you've
got the minimum exposure.

00:38:59.820 --> 00:39:02.820
What's more, since we've
charted this course

00:39:02.820 --> 00:39:06.340
of every environment
being its own GCP project,

00:39:06.340 --> 00:39:10.020
we might want to have a
central repository for things

00:39:10.020 --> 00:39:14.580
like build artifacts and
this service account,

00:39:14.580 --> 00:39:17.970
so that we can have one
central source of authority

00:39:17.970 --> 00:39:21.365
and then deploy into each
of those other environments.

00:39:21.365 --> 00:39:22.740
In that case,
we're going to need

00:39:22.740 --> 00:39:27.090
to collect all of the
different roles and permissions

00:39:27.090 --> 00:39:30.330
for the deployment process
of shipping resources,

00:39:30.330 --> 00:39:33.720
shipping Cloud Functions
into stage and production,

00:39:33.720 --> 00:39:36.480
and give that to the service
account on a cross project

00:39:36.480 --> 00:39:38.200
basis.

00:39:38.200 --> 00:39:40.650
This can be done by
identifying the email address

00:39:40.650 --> 00:39:42.150
of the service
account and adding it

00:39:42.150 --> 00:39:43.775
like a normal user
to other projects.

00:39:47.510 --> 00:39:49.880
For runtime secrets,
we've charted

00:39:49.880 --> 00:39:52.010
this course of using
a Cloud Storage

00:39:52.010 --> 00:39:54.140
bucket as a kind
of vault, switch

00:39:54.140 --> 00:39:57.920
we are going to have the
runtime function look up

00:39:57.920 --> 00:40:00.590
and pull in the
secrets as needed.

00:40:00.590 --> 00:40:03.190
Here, we have a little bit of
bash magic in the first couple

00:40:03.190 --> 00:40:06.620
lines of the slide, which is
looking up a Cloud Storage

00:40:06.620 --> 00:40:10.070
bucket that is specific
to an environment,

00:40:10.070 --> 00:40:15.080
using a little trick for a
dynamic variable names in bash.

00:40:15.080 --> 00:40:18.260
This bucket name is then passed
into the Function, or Cloud Run

00:40:18.260 --> 00:40:20.870
service, or App Engine
app when you deploy it,

00:40:20.870 --> 00:40:24.260
so that when those things start
up, they can load the secret

00:40:24.260 --> 00:40:27.292
and keep it in memory to
reuse for further requests

00:40:27.292 --> 00:40:27.875
in the future.

00:40:31.320 --> 00:40:35.970
Now, with all this talk of
giving permissions and roles

00:40:35.970 --> 00:40:40.742
to a service account with keys
exported to another system,

00:40:40.742 --> 00:40:42.450
there's a lot of access
that you're still

00:40:42.450 --> 00:40:44.220
exposing, even
though you're trying

00:40:44.220 --> 00:40:46.710
to get a least access approach.

00:40:46.710 --> 00:40:48.570
Being able to
deploy to production

00:40:48.570 --> 00:40:51.950
is not a trivial thing.

00:40:51.950 --> 00:40:55.450
And there are a number of
ways you might approach that.

00:40:55.450 --> 00:40:57.520
Since we're using
GitLab, you might

00:40:57.520 --> 00:41:01.570
take the OpenSource version of
GitLab and host that on GKE.

00:41:01.570 --> 00:41:05.470
I believe there's a code lab
session for that on Thursday,

00:41:05.470 --> 00:41:06.940
exploring how to do that.

00:41:06.940 --> 00:41:11.200
But one approach that Pari
and the Into the Woods team is

00:41:11.200 --> 00:41:14.500
exploring here is splitting up
the responsibilities of what

00:41:14.500 --> 00:41:15.990
different systems will do.

00:41:15.990 --> 00:41:19.210
GitLab can be responsible
for everything up to QA,

00:41:19.210 --> 00:41:22.630
and maybe Cloud Build will
handle all the operations

00:41:22.630 --> 00:41:24.400
for staging and production.

00:41:24.400 --> 00:41:28.330
Since so many of our operations
are wrapped up in bash scripts,

00:41:28.330 --> 00:41:31.520
they can be easily
executed by any runner.

00:41:31.520 --> 00:41:33.160
And so GitLab is
going to perform

00:41:33.160 --> 00:41:36.123
the same operations
as Cloud Build

00:41:36.123 --> 00:41:37.540
through the lens
of those scripts.

00:41:42.080 --> 00:41:44.510
And so as we've built out
the additional stages,

00:41:44.510 --> 00:41:47.570
we might even add deployment
and an acceptance stage

00:41:47.570 --> 00:41:49.110
for those end to end tests.

00:41:49.110 --> 00:41:52.430
Let's go ahead and see if that
demo from the start of the talk

00:41:52.430 --> 00:41:55.220
has completed and we have a
successfully deployed service.

00:42:09.480 --> 00:42:12.260
So here is the
GitLab CI pipeline

00:42:12.260 --> 00:42:13.500
that we started earlier.

00:42:13.500 --> 00:42:15.890
And now we see we have
some green checkboxes.

00:42:15.890 --> 00:42:19.220
And the last one here,
with the exclamation mark,

00:42:19.220 --> 00:42:22.850
looks like an accepted failure.

00:42:22.850 --> 00:42:24.770
But I'm not too worried
about that right now.

00:42:24.770 --> 00:42:26.990
I want to see, did
my QA environment

00:42:26.990 --> 00:42:29.750
get updated from this change?

00:42:29.750 --> 00:42:33.740
And looking into this terminal
output from the CI system,

00:42:33.740 --> 00:42:36.890
it looks like it ran
through all the steps,

00:42:36.890 --> 00:42:41.890
figured out an environment, and
deployed to a demo environment.

00:42:41.890 --> 00:42:45.580
And here, we have a URL to
look at our cloud run service.

00:43:04.330 --> 00:43:06.500
Well, that command
disappeared, didn't it?

00:43:12.030 --> 00:43:13.815
Well, since Cloud
Run services deploy

00:43:13.815 --> 00:43:15.800
in an authenticated
only way, and I

00:43:15.800 --> 00:43:21.320
don't have that command
handy, we will move on.

00:43:24.720 --> 00:43:26.134
Go back to the slides, please.

00:43:30.607 --> 00:43:32.100
Take it away.

00:43:32.100 --> 00:43:33.690
MARTIN OMANDER:
All right, so now

00:43:33.690 --> 00:43:37.590
that they have the whole CI/CD
pipeline set up, what have

00:43:37.590 --> 00:43:39.270
they learned along the way?

00:43:39.270 --> 00:43:41.580
Well first, Junior James,
he was a bit concerned

00:43:41.580 --> 00:43:44.760
about this CI/CD, there will
be a lot of bureaucracy.

00:43:44.760 --> 00:43:48.670
He's happy that CI/CD
actually helps him.

00:43:48.670 --> 00:43:51.120
Pari, who writes a
lot of these scripts,

00:43:51.120 --> 00:43:55.740
she sees that monorepo, that
adds complexity in the scripts,

00:43:55.740 --> 00:43:57.370
but it is worth it.

00:43:57.370 --> 00:43:59.625
Also, secrets management
is always tricky.

00:44:02.700 --> 00:44:07.350
Tess notes that serverless
makes the CI/CD easier

00:44:07.350 --> 00:44:09.840
because you can
test in environments

00:44:09.840 --> 00:44:14.500
that are exact carbon copies of
your production environments.

00:44:14.500 --> 00:44:16.500
And also, those environments
don't cost anything

00:44:16.500 --> 00:44:18.190
if they are not running.

00:44:18.190 --> 00:44:22.770
Finally, Sang is really happy
that they can release software

00:44:22.770 --> 00:44:23.700
more predictably.

00:44:23.700 --> 00:44:27.050
